= A6
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <internal energy> is the population-weighted sum of the molecular <energy levels>:
$$
U=\sum_i n_i\varepsilon_i=\frac Nq\sum_i\varepsilon_i e^{-\varepsilon_i/kT}.
$$
At fixed <volume>, assume the <energy levels> themselves are temperature-independent. Differentiating the molecular <partition function> gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial q}{\partial T}\right)_V
=\frac1{kT^2}\sum_i\varepsilon_i e^{-\varepsilon_i/kT},
\qquad\boxed{U=NkT^2\frac{\partial\log q}{\partial T}}.
$$
Choosing the zero of energy changes $U$ by the corresponding constant per molecule, so the ground-level convention must be consistent throughout.

= b
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Relative to the <ground state>, the <quantum harmonic oscillator> levels are $vhc\widetilde\omega$. Put $\theta_v=hc\widetilde\omega/k$. Summing the <geometric series> gives the <vibrational partition function> $q=(1-e^{-\theta_v/T})^{-1}$. Therefore the vibrational <internal energy> is
$$
\boxed{U=\frac{Nk\theta_v}{e^{\theta_v/T}-1}}
=\frac{Nhc\widetilde\omega}{e^{hc\widetilde\omega/kT}-1}.
$$
The omitted <zero-point energy> would add $Nhc\widetilde\omega/2$ if absolute oscillator energies were used.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

“Low” and “high” compare $kT$ with the vibrational spacing $hc\widetilde\omega$, not <temperature> with an arbitrary number of kelvin. For $T\ll\theta_v$, occupation of excited levels is exponentially suppressed and
$$
\boxed{U\sim Nk\theta_v e^{-\theta_v/T}\longrightarrow0}.
$$
This vanishing result uses the ground-relative energy zero. For $T\gg\theta_v$, expand $e^{\theta_v/T}-1\sim\theta_v/T$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{U\sim NkT}.
$$
This agrees with <equipartition>: the oscillator has one quadratic kinetic and one quadratic potential term, contributing $kT/2$ each. The corresponding limiting vibrational <heat capacity> is zero at low <temperature> and $Nk$ at high <temperature>.

= c
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Subtracting the $v=0$ energy in the <Morse oscillator> gives
$$
\frac{\varepsilon_v-\varepsilon_0}{hc}
=v\widetilde\omega-v(v+1)\widetilde\omega x.
$$
The first three relative wavenumbers are $0$, $213$, and $424\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$. Thus, with $\theta_1=213/0.695\ \mathrm K$ and $\theta_2=424/0.695\ \mathrm K$,
$$
\boxed{q_3(T)=1+e^{-\theta_1/T}+e^{-\theta_2/T}},\qquad
\boxed{q_3(100\ \mathrm K)=1.04891}.
$$
This is a three-level <partition function>, as stipulated, rather than an unrestricted sum of the anharmonic formula to arbitrarily large $v$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiating each <Boltzmann factor> yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial q_3}{\partial T}
=\frac{\theta_1e^{-\theta_1/T}+\theta_2e^{-\theta_2/T}}{T^2}}.
$$
The derivative is positive: warming increases occupation of both excited <energy levels>. At $100\ \mathrm K$,
$$
\boxed{\left.\frac{\partial q_3}{\partial T}\right|_{100\,\mathrm K}
=1.56692\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm K^{-1}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For one mole, $Nk=R$, so the three-level <Morse oscillator> result is
$$
\boxed{U_m=RT^2\frac{q_3'}{q_3}=124.21\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}}.
$$
For the full <quantum harmonic oscillator> with the given wavenumber,
$$
\boxed{U_{m,\mathrm{harm}}=\frac{R(215/0.695)}{e^{215/(0.695\cdot100)}-1}
=122.16\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}}.
$$
The specified anharmonic calculation is about $1.7\%$ higher. Anharmonicity lowers the first excitation spacings and increases excited-state occupation. Both results remain small because $100\ \mathrm K$ is well below the vibrational <temperature>. The comparison also mixes a three-level anharmonic truncation with an infinite harmonic sum; omitted higher Morse levels would make a further small positive correction at this <temperature>.

= A7
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For identical, non-interacting molecules, the translational <canonical partition function> is $Q_N=q_{\rm trans}^N/N!$. <Stirling's approximation> gives the <Helmholtz free energy>
$$
A=-NkT\left[\log(q_{\rm trans}/N)+1\right].
$$
At fixed $V$, $q_{\rm trans}\propto T^{3/2}$, so differentiation gives $S=Nk[\log(q_{\rm trans}/N)+5/2]$. For one mole at the standard-state molar <volume>,
$$
\boxed{S_{m,\rm trans}^{\circ}=R\left[\log\left(\frac{q_{m,\rm trans}^{\circ}}{N_A}\right)+\frac52\right]}.
$$
Here the quoted molar translational quantity is per mole, and its ratio to $N_A$ is dimensionless. The derivative defining <entropy> is taken at fixed <volume> before inserting the standard-state <volume>; differentiating along a constant-pressure path would give the wrong additive constant.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a linear <rigid rotor> in the high-temperature regime, $q_{\rm rot}\propto T$. Its molar rotational <entropy> is
$$
\boxed{S_{m,\rm rot}=R(\log q_{\rm rot}+1)}.
$$
The <rotational symmetry number> must already be included in $q_{\rm rot}$.

= b
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under the stated vibrational-ground-state approximation and a nondegenerate electronic <ground state>, only translation and rotation contribute. Therefore
$$
S_m^{\circ}=R\left[\log\left(\frac{7.966\times10^{30}}{N_A}\right)+\frac52+log(265.4)+1\right]
=\boxed{211.84\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}}.
$$
A temperature-independent ground-state energy, including <zero-point energy>, contributes no <entropy>. This estimate deliberately omits thermal vibrational excitation; it need not equal a tabulated value that includes all modes.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the same standard pressure and <temperature>, the same bond length, and the same electronic and vibrational-ground-state approximations. The <translational partition function> scales as <mass> to the power $3/2$, giving the factor $(47/44)^{3/2}$. For the original isotopologue, $I=32m_uR^2$ and the <rotational symmetry number> is two. For the isotope-substituted molecule, the two oxygen ends are distinguishable, so the symmetry number is one. Hence
$$
\frac{q_{\rm rot,new}}{q_{\rm rot,old}}=2\frac{33.91}{32}.
$$
The change in standard molar <entropy> is
$$
\Delta S_m=R\left[\frac32\log\frac{47}{44}+\log\left(2\frac{33.91}{32}\right)\right]
=7.0678\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}},
$$
so
$$
\boxed{S_{m,\rm new}^{\circ}\simeq218.91\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}}.
$$
This uses a convention omitting a separate nuclear-spin <entropy>. If nuclear-spin degeneracies are instead included explicitly, the spin-$1/2$ carbon-13 nucleus adds a further $R\log2$ relative to the spin-zero original isotopologue; that convention must be stated rather than silently mixed with the quoted <partition functions>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The substituted molecule has the larger <entropy>. Increased <mass> and <moment of inertia> reduce translational and rotational spacings and make more states thermally accessible. The largest contribution here is the loss of the twofold rotational indistinguishability of the oxygen ends, contributing $R\log2$. It is a <rotational symmetry number> correction for one isotope-defined species, not an <entropy of mixing> between different molecules.

= c
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

As a first estimate, neglect the small <mass> change and changes in geometry and vibrational populations. Removing an <electron> creates an unpaired spin, giving at least the spin-only electronic <entropy> increase
$$
\boxed{\Delta S_{m,\rm spin}\simeq R\log2=5.76\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}}.
$$
For a more specific estimate, the actual cation ground term is $X\,{}^2\Pi_g$, rather than an orbitally nondegenerate doublet. Ignoring <spin-orbit coupling>, its two orbital components and two spin components give electronic degeneracy four and
$$
\boxed{\Delta S_{m,\rm el}\simeq R\log4=11.53\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}}.
$$
The ground-state assignment is confirmed by state-resolved photoionization measurements.<Photoionization of CO2: Experimental Procedure by the National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Measurements restricted to channels leaving the cation in its X 2Πg ground electronic state]{ref} Spin-orbit splitting and changed vibrational/rotational constants refine this estimate; the question's supplied <partition functions> alone do not determine those corrections. The $R\log2$ answer is a spin-only estimate, while $R\log4$ additionally retains the orbital degeneracy.

